f 1053 #uuid "7adce642-b854-586d-bc06-8e89b32c3f0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167279 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (3x)\\sin (6x)dx =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7721acbc-048b-50e9-95a0-46bf90727bd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167281 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (3x)\\cos (6x)dx =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d707fb1-cb96-50fe-b7df-5abab3385ab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167925 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167925 #edb/ref 1061 594773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9048252a-25e5-52f9-bd7e-090c6a659879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167926 #edb/ref 1061 594774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58ba6660-e16f-5452-9a6a-841f7b3660e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167927 #edb/ref 1061 594777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "038580ae-a8f9-5da4-a4d6-a1de5dc8cfea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167928 #edb/ref 1061 594778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22c6f08d-2044-5989-8b6c-33807f441aef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167929 #edb/ref 1061 594781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a2daae3-51d3-5fc0-afb6-806b9e8b507e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167930 #edb/ref 1061 594782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2c70847-dbf9-50ac-a77c-3c64374510b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168040 #edb/ref 1064 "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(5x)\\operatorname{cos}(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}(5x)\\operatorname{cos}(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168040 #edb/ref 1065 14665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168040 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c26de959-d4f8-59d7-987a-b054cf26fe18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168040 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168041 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(7x)\\operatorname{sin}(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(7x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}(7x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}(7x)\\operatorname{sin}(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}(3x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}(11x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168041 #edb/ref 1065 14666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168041 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0b307dbf-5537-546f-8a0c-3ad325959aca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168041 #edb/ref 1067 "Integrating a Product of Sines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168042 #edb/ref 1064 "Next, when an integrand contains a product of sine and cosine, we can rewrite that product as a sum of sines using the sum and difference formulas for sine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) + \\sin(\\alpha-\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(8x)\\cos(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(8x)\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}(8x+2x)+\\operatorname{sin}(8x-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}(8x)\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}(10x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168042 #edb/ref 1065 14667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168042 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e3af3c5-49cd-524e-8614-b45a35212820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168042 #edb/ref 1067 "Integrating a Product of Sine and Cosine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312908 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312908 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312908 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7511559d-1260-514d-8be4-3e7cc538c779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313227 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313227 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313227 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f1be971-8240-5314-bbe0-c73114377645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313513 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313513 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313513 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3edf8c4-f1ba-521f-a323-8a7f41301969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313605 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d074817c-9881-518c-b1ea-75ba1846c23c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313675 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313675 #edb/